# C# Coding Standards

## Language & Framework

- Target: `net10.0`, LangVersion: `latest`
- Nullable reference types: **enabled** project-wide
- File-scoped namespaces: **required** (`namespace Foo;`, not `namespace Foo { }`)
- `TreatWarningsAsErrors: true` -- every warning is a build error
- `EnforceCodeStyleInBuild: true`
- `AnalysisLevel: latest-all` -- all .NET analyzers enabled

## Naming Conventions

- Private fields: `_camelCase` (e.g., `_viewHasher`)
- Interfaces: `I` prefix with PascalCase (e.g., `IManagedViewProvider`)
- Classes, methods, properties: PascalCase
- Parameters, local variables: camelCase
- Constants: PascalCase (e.g., `DefaultSchema`)

## Class Design

- Prefer `sealed` classes unless inheritance is explicitly intended
- One class per file (filename matches class name)
- Use `record` types for immutable data models
- Constructor parameters: validate with `ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull()`
- String parameters: validate with `ArgumentException.ThrowIfNullOrWhiteSpace()`

## XML Documentation

All public types and members **must** have XML docs:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Discovers managed view definitions from embedded SQL files.
/// </summary>
/// <param name="assembly">The assembly containing embedded SQL resources.</param>
/// <returns>A collection of discovered view definitions.</returns>
public IReadOnlyList<IManagedViewDefinition> Discover(Assembly assembly)
```

## Error Handling

Use the custom exception hierarchy rooted at `ManagedViewException`:
- `ManagedViewSqlParseException` -- SQL parsing failures
- `ManagedViewProviderException` -- provider-specific errors
- `ManagedViewDependencyCycleException` -- circular view dependencies
- `ManagedViewDuplicateException` -- duplicate view names
- `ManagedViewNotFoundException` -- view not found

Never throw raw `Exception` or `ApplicationException`.

## Dependencies

- Core package: no unnecessary dependencies beyond EF Core
- Provider packages: only depend on core + their database driver
- Use `TryAddSingleton` / `TryAddScoped` to allow DI overrides
